WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.910
How many voters will cast their vote this
November on the basis of a candidate’s position

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.629
on abortion?

00:00:11.629 --> 00:00:15.390
Survey data collected by several pollsters
provides a rough gauge.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:20.440
When asked by Gallup how the abortion issue
might affect their vote, around 2 in 10 Americans

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.070
answer that a candidate MUST share their views
on the issue.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.140
That’s a significant single-issue bloc.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.430
Nearly half, however, say that abortion will be
one of many important factors, while 3 in

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.510
10 say abortion won’t be a factor for them.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:41.110
The responses have been fairly consistent
in Gallup’s surveys for more than two decades.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.190
Quinnipiac University phrases its question
differently from Gallup’s.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:50.700
In their latest poll from December 2017, 64
percent of registered voters said that they

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.970
could still vote for a candidate if they did
not agree with the candidate on the issue

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.580
of abortion, while 29 percent said they could
not.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:02.180
On abortion questions generally, men and women
do not differ.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.680
For most Americans, then, abortion is not
a litmus test.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.640
Will abortion be a litmus test issue for you in November? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.180
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.920
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

